.TH wifi 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
wifi \- join a wireless network, or report on the radio
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wifi
.br
.B wifi \-f
.br
.B wifi
.I ssid key
[\fItype\fR]
.br
.B wifi \-d
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no arguments,
.B wifi
reports what the radio is doing: whether it is associated, the signal
strength, and the address, mask, gateway and nameserver of the DHCP
lease.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Join the network named in
.BR /etc/wifi.conf ,
take a DHCP lease, and write
.B /etc/resolv.conf
from it.  Writing resolv.conf is what makes names work, so this is the
form to use.
.TP
.I "ssid key" \fR[\fItype\fR]
Join without using the file.  Nothing is saved.  Neither the SSID nor
the key may contain a space.
.TP
.B \-d
Disconnect.
.SH THE CONFIGURATION FILE
.B /etc/wifi.conf
holds one line of three fields: the SSID, the key, and the
authentication type \- 0 open, 1 WPA-TKIP, 2 WPA2-AES, 3 WPA2 mixed.
Nearly every home network wants 2.
.PP
The file holds the key in plain text.  It ships mode 600 and
.B wifi
complains if it finds otherwise, but anyone who can read the card can
read the key.
.SH NOTES
Bringing the radio up stops the machine for about half a second: the
kernel uploads 230K of firmware to the chip, and a FUZIX system call is
not interrupted, so nothing else runs while it happens \- including the
keyboard.  Joining the network afterwards does not block.
.PP
A Pico Computer 2 has no radio; those pins are its SD card and its LED.
The same kernel runs on both and says
.B "no radio (Pico Computer 2)"
rather than failing.
.PP
To join at boot, put
.B wifi \-f
in
.BR /etc/rc .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/wifi.conf
.TP
.B /etc/resolv.conf
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ping (1),
.BR dig (1),
.BR ntpdate (1),
.BR htget (1),
.BR httpd (1)
